There appears to be some kernels in PyTorch that have a recurring pattern of creating temporary intermediate tensors for the sake of correctness (e.g. when the provided output tensor has "wrong" strides).
When making these kernels structured, we use set_output(sizes, strides, ...)  inside their META function for re-allocating the output tensors. However, that alone won't guarantee that the output tensors were restrided, since they only are restrided when they have different sizes than what it was expected.
That makes so that kernel implementers have to:

[META] use set_output
[IMPL] check if they were actually restrided
[IMPL] create intermediate tensors with the right strides
[IMPL] compute and write results to the intermediate tensors
[IMPL] copy the result back from the intermediate tensors.

Structured kernels codegen could take care of most of this (2, 3, and 5) automatically, while enforcing correctness. For example:
TORCH_META_FUNC(kernel)(const Tensor& self) {
    auto strides = contiguous_strides(self.sizes(), /*f-contig =*/true);
    set_output(0, self.sizes(), strides, self.options(), {})
}

TORCH_IMPL_FUNC(kernel_out)(const Tensor& self, const Tensor& out) {
    // 2. No need to check the strides
    // 3. No need to create (explicitly) an intermediate output tensor
    // auto out_ = clone_if_not_fcontig(self);

    /* computation */

    // 5. No need to copy results back
    // if (!is_fcontig(out)) {
    //     out.copy_(out_);
    // }
}
See #67015 (comment)
